From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 22:10:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] DIAGNOSTIC: net: ipa: expose modem GSI state and mem_offset

Not for source= beyond the investigation it belongs to.

An LTE attach kills this SoC about ten seconds after the modem finishes
programming its GSI channels, and the interval is constant across runs, so
something expires. Everything the AP can see has been checked and is clean:
the data path carries real traffic on GSM, runtime PM is not it, no QMI
message goes unanswered, no GSI or IPA error interrupt fires, and rmtfs never
stalls. Whatever is waiting is on the modem's side.

One piece of the modem's state is readable from here. GSI banks its registers
per execution environment, and mainline only ever addresses its own:

	REG_STRIDE_FIELDS(CH_C_CNTXT_0, ch_c_cntxt_0,
			  0x0000f000 + 0x4000 * GSI_EE_AP, 0x80);

The modem's bank is one stride further along, and gsi->virt is the raw
mapping, so its channel contexts are at 0xf000 + 0x4000 * GSI_EE_MODEM +
0x80 * channel, with CHSTATE in bits 20-23 decoding as the AP's does.

Export them so userspace can sample the file while the attach runs. A modem
channel sitting in error, or stuck mid-transition, would say where the ten
seconds go.

Taking a runtime PM reference is not tidiness here: a register read against a
gated GSI does not come back, and the AP suspends IPA immediately after the
attach, which is exactly when this is worth reading.

Also export ipa->mem_offset. Every address handed to the modem over QMI is
built on it, and two sizes were built on it as well until 0079, so whether it
is zero decides whether that mistake ever mattered.
---
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_sysfs.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/ipa_sysfs.c
@@ -3,6 +3,8 @@
 /* Copyright (C) 2021-2024 Linaro Ltd. */
 
 #include <linux/device.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/sysfs.h>
 #include <linux/types.h>
 
@@ -58,8 +60,71 @@
 
 static DEVICE_ATTR_RO(version);
 
+/* DIAGNOSTIC: the modem's GSI channel states, one hex digit each.
+ *
+ * GSI banks its registers per execution environment and mainline only ever
+ * addresses its own, so the modem's contexts sit one 0x4000 stride further
+ * along. CHSTATE is bits 20-23 and decodes as the AP's does: 1 allocated,
+ * 2 started, 3 stopped, 4 stopping, 0xf error.
+ */
+#define GSI_CH_C_CNTXT_0_RAW(ee, ch)	(0xf000 + 0x4000 * (ee) + 0x80 * (ch))
+#define GSI_DIAG_CHANNEL_COUNT		16
+
+static ssize_t
+modem_gsi_state_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr,
+		     char *buf)
+{
+	struct ipa *ipa = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	void __iomem *virt = ipa->gsi.virt;
+	size_t len = 0;
+	u32 ch;
+	int ret;
+
+	/* A read against a gated GSI does not return, and the AP suspends IPA
+	 * right after the attach, which is when this is worth reading.
+	 */
+	ret = pm_runtime_get_sync(dev);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		pm_runtime_put_noidle(dev);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	for (ch = 0; ch < GSI_DIAG_CHANNEL_COUNT; ch++) {
+		u32 val = ioread32(virt +
+				   GSI_CH_C_CNTXT_0_RAW(GSI_EE_MODEM, ch));
+
+		len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "%x", (val >> 20) & 0xf);
+	}
+	len += sysfs_emit_at(buf, len, "\n");
+
+	pm_runtime_put(dev);
+
+	return len;
+}
+
+static DEVICE_ATTR_RO(modem_gsi_state);
+
+/* DIAGNOSTIC: the shared memory base offset the hardware reports.
+ *
+ * Every address handed to the modem over QMI is built on this. Two sizes were
+ * built on it as well until 0079; if this reads zero that mistake was
+ * harmless, and if it does not, the modem was told its statistics regions were
+ * this many bytes larger than they are.
+ */
+static ssize_t
+mem_offset_show(struct device *dev, struct device_attribute *attr, char *buf)
+{
+	struct ipa *ipa = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+
+	return sysfs_emit(buf, "0x%08x\n", ipa->mem_offset);
+}
+
+static DEVICE_ATTR_RO(mem_offset);
+
 static struct attribute *ipa_attrs[] = {
 	&dev_attr_version.attr,
+	&dev_attr_modem_gsi_state.attr,
+	&dev_attr_mem_offset.attr,
 	NULL
 };
 
